  {"title":"Bass Split & Slammed Sub","page":"Bass Split & Slammed Sub","file":"bass.html","anchor":"","text":"Mono sub that never moves under 180 Hz . Grit and pick above it. BASS BUS peaks at -8 to -6 dBFS ."},
  {"title":"DjinnBass & Bonecrusher source","page":"Bass Split & Slammed Sub","file":"bass.html","anchor":"source","text":"DjinnBass Alternate picking 1/16 (1/8 on mid-tempo) Amplifier Power OFF (clean DI into Bonecrusher) Velocity steady 110-118 unverified Velocity accents 124-127 unverified Keyswitch Pick (default) C-2 Keyswitch Dead Mute G-2 Keyswitch Slide Down D#-2 slow / E-2 fast Keyswitch Slide Up F-2 slow / F#-2 fast Amplifier Power OFF bypasses the Pre-Processed Tones. Bonecrusher gets a clean DI. Route post-DI/Bonecrusher"},
  {"title":"The 180 Hz split","page":"Bass Split & Slammed Sub","file":"bass.html","anchor":"split","text":"Treat the bass as two instruments: a mono sub and a biting mid guitar. Why 180 Hz Detail Kick window Kick punch sits at 50-70 Hz , resonance to 100-120 Hz . The sub band holds the bass fundamentals below the low-mid box. Guitar chug Palm-mute cab thump lives at 120-160 Hz . A 180 Hz ceiling keeps the sub"},
  {"title":"Zero-latency split & polarity check","page":"Bass Split & Slammed Sub","file":"bass.html","anchor":"polarity","text":"Use minimum-phase filters 24 dB/oct : TDR Nova HP/LP Slope, or two stacked ReaEQ pass bands. They replace ReaFIR. Zero latency, sharp sub transients. Play a sustained low note Keep BASS LOW and BASS HIGH both active. Flip polarity on one band Use the track Phase button, or JS: Utility/chanmix2 Left Phase and Right Phase = Invert. Keep the thicker"},
  {"title":"BASS LOW (<180 Hz) chain","page":"Bass Split & Slammed Sub","file":"bass.html","anchor":"bass-low","text":"Six slots. Slammed sub, harmonic floor, kick duck last. 1 ReaEQ REAPER FILTER LPF 24 dB/oct @ 180 Hz (two stacked Low Pass bands) unverified HPF 24 dB/oct @ 30-35 Hz (two stacked High Pass bands) unverified Isolates the sub fundamentals at zero latency. The HPF sits below the 49 Hz low G. At 40-45 Hz it would cut the"},
  {"title":"SC_KICK into BASS LOW","page":"Bass Split & Slammed Sub","file":"bass.html","anchor":"sc-kick-setup","text":"Set 4 channels On BASS LOW , set Track Channels to 4 . Send from SC_KICK Pre-Fader (Post-FX). Audio 1/2 to 3/4 . Set the detector ReaComp Detector input = Auxiliary Input L+R . Full hub rules on Routing ."},
  {"title":"BASS HIGH (>180 Hz) chain","page":"Bass Split & Slammed Sub","file":"bass.html","anchor":"bass-high","text":"1 TDR Nova Tokyo Dawn DYN EQ HPF 24 dB/oct @ 180 Hz Dynamic band -2 to -3 dB @ 2-3 kHz Band Q ~2.5 Ratio 3:1 Attack / Release 3 ms / 50 ms Strips sub energy. Tames fret clank only when it spikes. 2 Bassforge Bonecrusher JST AMP Amp Bonecrusher Crush Up until grit fills 800 Hz -"},
  {"title":"BASS BUS glue","page":"Bass Split & Slammed Sub","file":"bass.html","anchor":"bass-bus","text":"Sums BASS LOW + BASS HIGH . Peak target -8 to -6 dBFS . 1 JST BG-Bass JST GLUE Method Low + High Threshold + Comp Set for 2-3 dB GR Mix 100% Output 0 dB (unity) Welds the sub floor and Bonecrusher clank into one instrument. No baked-in EQ. The vendor manual lists Method as Low, High, or Low"},
  {"title":"Set the bass level against the vocal","page":"Bass Split & Slammed Sub","file":"bass.html","anchor":"bass-balance","text":"Lock kick and bass first Kick and bass should hit together as one low-end event. The SC_KICK duck keeps the kick on top. Dovetail bass top with guitar bottom Bendeth matched the bass top end to the guitar bottom end. Where the guitar HPF ends, the bass grit takes over. Check the vocal last Play a chorus. Move BASS BUS"},
  {"title":"Problem fix","page":"Bass Split & Slammed Sub","file":"bass.html","anchor":"problems","text":"Problem Fix Tool Bass thin at the split Flip polarity on one band. Keep the thicker result at 180 Hz . Track Phase / JS: Utility/chanmix2 Sub level jumps note to note FG-116 6-10 dB GR , then ReaLimit -12.00 dB . FG-116 + ReaLimit Kick buried by the sub Duck 2.5-3.5 dB from SC_KICK , after ReaLimit. ReaComp Duck"},
  {"title":"Calculators","page":"Calculators","file":"calculators.html","anchor":"","text":"Turn tempo, gain, loudness and tuning into numbers you type into REAPER. Every value updates as you type."},
  {"title":"Tempo to time","page":"Calculators","file":"calculators.html","anchor":"tempo","text":"Quarter note = 60000 / BPM ms. Dotted 1.5 . Triplet 2/3 . Tempo (BPM) Quarter note Note values (ms / Hz) Note Straight ms Hz Dotted ms Hz Triplet ms Hz"},
  {"title":"Tempo-synced starting points","page":"Calculators","file":"calculators.html","anchor":"tempo-derived","text":"Reverb and release (starting point) Pre-delay 1/64 - Pre-delay 1/128 - Decay to 1/4 - Decay to 1/2 - Decay to 1 bar (4/4) - Comp release 1/8 - Comp release 1/16 - Decay = note length minus the 1/64 pre-delay, so the tail ends on the next note. Release on the 1/8 or 1/16 lets the compressor recover before"},
  {"title":"Gain","page":"Calculators","file":"calculators.html","anchor":"gain","text":"Linear amplitude = 10^(dB / 20) . Type in either box. dB and linear Gain (dB) Linear amplitude GClip clip level Clip (%) Level (dBFS) dBFS = 20 log10(% / 100) . The mix bus parks GClip at 71% , about -3.0 dBFS . Stacked gain changes Changes in dB, separated by spaces or commas Stages - Total - Net"},
  {"title":"Loudness and streaming","page":"Calculators","file":"calculators.html","anchor":"loudness","text":"Enter the master's integrated loudness and true peak. The table shows how far each platform turns it down. The site prints at -8.5 to -7.5 LUFS , about -8 LUFS , with true peak at or below -1.0 dBTP . Integrated (LUFS) True peak (dBTP) Platform normalization for the entered loudness Platform Normalizes to Change Targets are reference values for"},
  {"title":"Tuning to frequencies","page":"Calculators","file":"calculators.html","anchor":"tuning","text":"Equal temperament, A4 = 440 Hz . Strings run low (7) to high (1). Tuning Lowest note String notes and frequencies for the selected tuning String Instrument Note Hz"},
  {"title":"HPF zones","page":"Calculators","file":"calculators.html","anchor":"tuning-hpf","text":"Filter zones (starting point) Guitar HPF floor - Guitar 2nd harmonic - Rhythm HPF (tone) - Bass HPF (sub floor) - Bass HPF below low note - Guitar HPF floor = 0.9 the low note. It clears rumble and keeps the fundamental. Bass and rhythm values come from the Bass and Guitars pages."},
  {"title":"Note and Hz","page":"Calculators","file":"calculators.html","anchor":"note-hz","text":"Use it to tune kick and snare samples, or to name a resonance you found with a sweep. Note to Hz Note Octave Frequency - MIDI note - Hz to nearest note Frequency (Hz) Nearest note - Offset - Note exact - Octave numbers are scientific pitch (A4 = 440 Hz, C4 = MIDI 60). Kontakt names the same notes"},
  {"title":"7-Phase Album Checklist","page":"7-Phase Album Checklist","file":"checklist.html","anchor":"","text":"Run every song through the same seven phases. Ticks reset on reload."},
  {"title":"Phase 1: gain staging, routing, mix bus","page":"7-Phase Album Checklist","file":"checklist.html","anchor":"phase-1","text":"Tracks: -12 to -10 dBFS Gain Staging Routing Park all faders at 0.00 dB unity. Trim clip gain and VSTi outputs (GGD, DjinnBass, ANA 2, LABS) to -12 to -10 dBFS peak. Verify two-branch routing: INSTRUMENT BUS + VOCAL MASTER > PRINT / MIX BUS . Uncheck Master/Parent Send on SC_KICK , SC_SNARE , SC_SPOTLIGHT . Load the parked chain:"},
  {"title":"Phase 2: drum foundation","page":"7-Phase Album Checklist","file":"checklist.html","anchor":"phase-2","text":"DRUM BUS: -8 to -6 dBFS Drums Sample-align GGD M&M + Invasion triggers. Max Turbo unverified . No close-mic gates. Carve Kick: HPF 42-45 Hz , +2 to +3 dB @ 65-75 Hz , -4 to -5 dB @ 280-350 Hz . Carve single Snare: HPF 100-120 Hz , +2 to +3 dB @ 200-220 Hz , +2 dB @"},
  {"title":"Phase 3: bass split & slammed sub","page":"7-Phase Album Checklist","file":"checklist.html","anchor":"phase-3","text":"BASS BUS: -8 to -6 dBFS Bass Set DjinnBass: 1/16 or 1/8 Alternate Picking, Amplifier Power OFF. Feed Bonecrusher. Check polarity at the 180 Hz split. BASS LOW : 24 dB/oct LPF 180 Hz + HPF 30-35 Hz (stacked ReaEQ bands) > 100% mono > JST EQ. BASS LOW : Revival Thickness 15-25% > FG-116 6-10 dB GR . BASS"},
  {"title":"Phase 4: guitars + first-chorus dial","page":"7-Phase Album Checklist","file":"checklist.html","anchor":"phase-4","text":"INSTRUMENT BUS: -6 to -5 dBFS Guitars Step B Bring up RHYTHM GTR BUS : 100% dry, hard-panned. Rhythm chain: JST EQ (HPF 85-100 Hz , LPF 7.5-9 kHz ) > JW BG-Guitars (Chug Slayer ON, Crush 2-3 dB GR , never more) > Soothe2 ( 1.5-2.0 kHz low cut band). Loop a loud chorus. Pull PRINT / MIX BUS"},
  {"title":"Phase 5: lead vocals & BGVs","page":"7-Phase Album Checklist","file":"checklist.html","anchor":"phase-5","text":"VOCAL MASTER: -6 to -5 dBFS Vocals LEAD VOX BUS : E2Deesser > JST EQ HPF 85-100 Hz > Coffee The Pun (germanium preamp ON unverified ). LEAD VOX BUS : FG-116 Vintage 4-6 dB > VM Custom Opto 3-5 dB , never above 6 dB GR . LEAD VOX BUS : Soothe2 ( 350-400 Hz low cut band, Depth"},
  {"title":"Phase 6: synths, strings, FX automation","page":"7-Phase Album Checklist","file":"checklist.html","anchor":"phase-6","text":"PRINT / MIX BUS: -4 to -3 dBFS Synths & SFX Orchestral FX Returns Fade up ANA 2: 0% arp/bass reverb, 0-2 dB GR max compression, SC_KICK duck 2-3 dB on sub drops. Fade up Spitfire LABS strings: 0-15% reverb, VM Custom Opto 3-5 dB GR . Strings: Mid/Side JST EQ, HPF 150-180 Hz (violins, violas, pads) or 100-120 Hz"},
  {"title":"Phase 7: tilt sweep, GClip, master print","page":"7-Phase Album Checklist","file":"checklist.html","anchor":"phase-7","text":"MASTER: -8.5 to -7.5 LUFS Final print Sweep JST Max #1 Tone (Dark Light unverified ) at 0.0 dB GR . Engage Soothe2 ( 250 Hz low cut band, Depth 0.8-1.2 ) only if needed. Set GClip to shave 1.0-2.0 dB peaks. Switch every Soothe2 instance to 4x Oversample unverified . Push JST Max #2 (Rock preset) to 2.5-4.5 dB"},
  {"title":"Drums & DRUM BUS","page":"Drums & DRUM BUS","file":"drums.html","anchor":"","text":"Punchy kick, cracking snare, tame cymbals. DRUM BUS peaks at -8 to -6 dBFS ."},
  {"title":"Source setup","page":"Drums & DRUM BUS","file":"drums.html","anchor":"source","text":"Source: GGD Modern & Massive plus Invasion triggers at Max Turbo unverified . One snare shell Keep a single natural snare. No multi-snare stacking. Sample-align triggers Align the Invasion snare trigger to the shell. Ghost notes stay organic. Skip close-mic gates and compressors GGD at Max Turbo already controls bleed and dynamics. Feed the crush from shells only Kick, Snare"},
  {"title":"KICK BUS","page":"Drums & DRUM BUS","file":"drums.html","anchor":"kick","text":"M&M Sum/Sub + Invasion trigger. 1 ReaEQ REAPER EQ HPF 42-45 Hz, 24 dB/oct (two stacked High Pass bands) unverified Punch +2 to +3 dB @ 65-75 Hz, Bandwidth 1.0 oct (about Q 1.4) Boxiness -4 to -5 dB @ 280-350 Hz Beater +2.5 to +3.5 dB @ 3.2-4 kHz The HPF clears room for the 49-58 Hz bass fundamental."},
  {"title":"SNARE BUS","page":"Drums & DRUM BUS","file":"drums.html","anchor":"snare","text":"Single snare + Invasion trigger. 1 ReaEQ REAPER EQ HPF 100-120 Hz, 24 dB/oct (two stacked High Pass bands) unverified Body +2 to +3 dB @ 200-220 Hz Ring -3 dB @ 550-700 Hz Crack +2 dB @ 2.8-3.2 kHz Air +1.5 dB @ 8 kHz Body and crack up, ring out. The HPF sits higher than a standard-tuning mix."},
  {"title":"TOMS BUS","page":"Drums & DRUM BUS","file":"drums.html","anchor":"toms","text":"1 ReaEQ REAPER EQ HPF rack 75 Hz HPF floor 55 Hz Mud -4 dB @ 380-450 Hz Attack +2.5 dB @ 4.5 kHz Clears mud and brings stick attack forward. Alternatives SLATE FG-S 2 kHs Transient Shaper Kilohearts TRANS Sustain -20% to -35% unverified Attack +10-15% unverified Shortens ring and bleed with no gate. Fills stay clean. Alternatives STOCK"},
  {"title":"OVERHEADS / CYMBALS","page":"Drums & DRUM BUS","file":"drums.html","anchor":"overheads","text":"1 ReaEQ REAPER EQ HPF 400-450 Hz, 24 dB/oct (two stacked High Pass bands) unverified Shelf +1 dB @ 12 kHz+ The steep HPF protects snare phase. Raise the HPF until the cymbals start to thin, then back it off a little. Published metal mixes run it up to 550 Hz. Alternatives SLATE FG-N SLATE Fresh Air, High Air 10%"},
  {"title":"ROOM BUS","page":"Drums & DRUM BUS","file":"drums.html","anchor":"room","text":"1 ReaEQ REAPER EQ HPF 150-185 Hz LPF 6.5-7.5 kHz Cut -3 dB @ 300 Hz Keeps the room out of the low end and the cymbal wash. A crushed room at 100 Hz smears the kick under Drop G bass. Mynett high-passes the room at 185 Hz to protect kick punch. If the cymbals still wash, lower the LPF"},
  {"title":"Parallel & plate returns","page":"Drums & DRUM BUS","file":"drums.html","anchor":"returns","text":"Both returns sum into DRUM BUS . Placement rules live on Routing ."},
  {"title":"SNARE PLATE","page":"Drums & DRUM BUS","file":"drums.html","anchor":"snare-plate","text":"1 Lustrous Plates LiquidSonics VERB Plate Steel Damper (reverb length) Short, about 0.6-0.9 s tail unverified Pre-delay 10-20 ms Low cut / High cut 220 Hz / 7 kHz Short bright tail that cuts through the guitars. Automate it up in breakdowns. Published metal snare plates run 0.5-0.85 s with 7-11 ms pre-delay. Fast double-kick parts blur with a longer"},
  {"title":"DRUM PARALLEL CRUSH","page":"Drums & DRUM BUS","file":"drums.html","anchor":"parallel-crush","text":"Fed post-fader by Kick, Snare and Tom acoustic shells only. 1 VM FG-401 Slate COMP Ratio 10:1 unverified Target GR 12-18 dB Blend Under dry kit, start -14 to -18 dB Adds density and sustain. The dry kit keeps the transient. A documented modern-metal crush ran about 18 dB GR, fed by kick, snare and toms, never cymbals. Heavier GR"},
  {"title":"DRUM BUS master","page":"Drums & DRUM BUS","file":"drums.html","anchor":"drum-bus","text":"Peak target: -8 to -6 dBFS . 1 ReaEQ REAPER EQ Low +1 dB @ 65 Hz Low-mid -1.5 dB @ 320 Hz Top +1 dB @ 10 kHz Broad kit tilt. Small moves only. Alternatives JST JST EQ SLATE CS-EQ 2 VBC FG-Grey Slate COMP Ratio 4:1 unverified Attack 30 ms unverified Release Auto HPF (detector) 90 Hz Target"},
  {"title":"Problem fix","page":"Drums & DRUM BUS","file":"drums.html","anchor":"problems","text":"Problem Fix Tool Kick and bass fight below 60 Hz Kick HPF 42-45 Hz . Punch at 65-75 Hz . ReaEQ Kick and bass still mask each other Move the punch boost up to 75-80 Hz . Do not boost the kick below 75 Hz in this tuning. ReaEQ Kick sounds like cardboard Cut -4 to -5 dB at 280-350"},
  {"title":"FX Returns & soothe2 Matrix","page":"FX Returns & soothe2 Matrix","file":"fx-returns.html","anchor":"","text":"Six named returns build one shared space. Every soothe2 instance runs with a raised low cut band. Never full-band default."},
  {"title":"Why shared returns","page":"FX Returns & soothe2 Matrix","file":"fx-returns.html","anchor":"why-shared","text":"One space Drums, vocals and guitars share the same reverbs. The ear hears one room. Phase coherence Separate stereo reverbs on hard-panned rhythms wash out of phase across the center. They mask the snare and the vocal. Ducking A return can duck from a key. Tails drop 3-5 dB during fast parts and bloom in the gaps."},
  {"title":"Six named returns","page":"FX Returns & soothe2 Matrix","file":"fx-returns.html","anchor":"returns","text":"Return Fed by Destination & ducking DRUM PARALLEL CRUSH Kick, snare & tom shells only (no OH or triggers) Into DRUM BUS SNARE PLATE SNARE BUS + subtle toms ( -8 to -10 dB ) Into DRUM BUS VOX & LEAD PLATE LEAD VOX, BGVs, LEAD GTR BUS Inside VOCAL MASTER . Ducked 2-3 dB by SC_SPOTLIGHT VOX MICRO-PITCH &"},
  {"title":"DRUM PARALLEL CRUSH","page":"FX Returns & soothe2 Matrix","file":"fx-returns.html","anchor":"drum-crush","text":"1 VM FG-401 Slate COMP Ratio 10:1 unverified Target GR 12-18 dB Blend Under dry kit, start -14 to -18 dB Hard crush on shells only. Same rack as the Drums page. Blend it under the dry kit for density. Alternatives SLATE FG-116 Vintage, All-Buttons unverified SLATE VBC FG-MU, -8 to -12 dB GR JST JW BG-Drums, Pumpin&#39; STOCK ReaComp"},
  {"title":"SNARE PLATE","page":"FX Returns & soothe2 Matrix","file":"fx-returns.html","anchor":"snare-plate","text":"1 Lustrous Plates Slate VERB Plate Steel Damper (decay) Short, about 0.6-0.9 s by ear Pre-delay 10-20 ms Low cut 220 Hz High cut 7 kHz A dense, dark plate gives the snare size without washing over guitars. The manual describes Steel as Chrome with a shorter low band decay. That keeps the low mids clear under 7-string chugs. Alternatives"},
  {"title":"VOX & LEAD PLATE","page":"FX Returns & soothe2 Matrix","file":"fx-returns.html","anchor":"vox-plate","text":"1 Lustrous Plates Slate VERB Plate Silver / Gold Damper (decay) Medium, about 1.4-1.8 s by ear Pre-delay 30-45 ms Low cut 250 Hz High cut 6 kHz Dynamics: Reverb ducking 2-3 dB, external sidechain SC_SPOTLIGHT Dynamics: Attack Fast Dynamics: Release Set so the tail swells back between phrases. Starting point. Pre-delay keeps consonants dry. The duck lets the tail"},
  {"title":"VOX MICRO-PITCH & SLAP","page":"FX Returns & soothe2 Matrix","file":"fx-returns.html","anchor":"slap","text":"1 Repeater Slate DELAY Pan L / Pan R Both near center (mono / narrow) Delay L / R 85-115 ms Feedback L / R 0% HPF L / R, LPF L / R 300 Hz, 3 kHz One short repeat thickens the dry front-row vocal body. Alternatives STOCK ReaDelay OTHER SSL X-Delay 2 kHs Pitch Shifter (optional) Kilohearts PITCH"},
  {"title":"STEREO TEMPO DELAY","page":"FX Returns & soothe2 Matrix","file":"fx-returns.html","anchor":"tempo-delay","text":"1 Repeater Slate DELAY Delay L / R 1/4 or 1/8 dotted Ping-Pong ON Feedback L / R 20-30% HPF L / R, LPF L / R 300 Hz, 3.5 kHz Duck (compressor after Repeater) 3-4 dB by SC_SPOTLIGHT The HPF and LPF keep repeats out of the 7-string low mids and the cymbals. Repeater has no ducker, so a"},
  {"title":"WIDE MIX HALL","page":"FX Returns & soothe2 Matrix","file":"fx-returns.html","anchor":"wide-hall","text":"1 VerbSuite Classics (hall) Slate VERB Device type Hall Decay 2.0-2.8 s Pre-delay 40-60 ms HPF (ReaEQ after the reverb) 200 Hz LPF (ReaEQ after the reverb) 5.5 kHz One album backdrop for strings, pads, octave guitars and BGVs. VerbSuite has only a 3-band Reverb EQ, with no HPF or LPF, so a ReaEQ follows it. Alternatives JST JST Sky"},
  {"title":"Pre-delay & decay from tempo","page":"FX Returns & soothe2 Matrix","file":"fx-returns.html","anchor":"tempo-math","text":"One quarter note in ms is 60000 / BPM . Divide for shorter notes. Tempo-timed pre-delay and decay make tails move with the song. BPM 1/64 1/32 1/16 1 bar (4/4) 100 38 ms 75 ms 150 ms 2.40 s 120 31 ms 63 ms 125 ms 2.00 s 140 27 ms 54 ms 107 ms 1.71 s 160 23"},
  {"title":"Global soothe2 matrix","page":"FX Returns & soothe2 Matrix","file":"fx-returns.html","anchor":"soothe2-matrix","text":"Bus / track Low cut band Depth & mode Controls / protects LEAD VOX BUS 350-400 Hz 2.5-4.5 Soft / Hard Tames 2.5-4.5 kHz throat bite and sibilance. Protects chest body. OVERHEADS / CYMBALS 1.5-2.0 kHz 2.0-3.5 Soft Smooths crash wash. Leaves snare bleed untouched. Never on shells. RHYTHM GTR BUS 1.5-2.0 kHz 2.0-3.0 Soft Removes 3.5-4.5 kHz fizz. Protects"},
  {"title":"soothe2 rules","page":"FX Returns & soothe2 Matrix","file":"fx-returns.html","anchor":"soothe2-rules","text":"Never leave the low cut band at 20 Hz The low cut band shapes what soothe2 reacts to. Full-band soothe2 reacts to sub and chugs. It pumps and guts the low end. Soft by default Soft keeps transients. Use Hard only for static artifacts, like a 3.2 kHz cab whistle. Oversample on render Set the offline rendering quality higher than"},
  {"title":"Problem fix","page":"FX Returns & soothe2 Matrix","file":"fx-returns.html","anchor":"problems","text":"Problem Fix Tool Reverb wash masks the vocal Duck the plate 2-3 dB from SC_SPOTLIGHT . Lustrous Plates / SC_SPOTLIGHT Delay clutters guitar low mids HPF 300 Hz , LPF 3.5 kHz on both channels. Repeater Hall muddies the low end HPF 200 Hz after VerbSuite. On Sky Box, turn Mono Low End ON. ReaEQ / Sky Box Cymbals warble"},
  {"title":"Gain Staging & Top-Down Calibration","page":"Gain Staging & Top-Down Calibration","file":"gain-staging.html","anchor":"","text":"Feed every analog-modeled plugin -18 dBFS RMS ( -12 to -10 dBFS peaks). Faders stay at 0.00 dB unity."},
  {"title":"Why gain stage","page":"Gain Staging & Top-Down Calibration","file":"gain-staging.html","anchor":"why","text":"Analog-modeled plugins expect -18 dBFS RMS . That equals about -12 to -10 dBFS peaks. This applies to: Slate VCC Coffee The Pun VBC FG-Grey JW/JST Bus Glue The Slate manuals state the reference. VCC: 0 VU = -18 dBFS (1 kHz sine). Revival: 0 VU at a -18 dBFS peak sine. VTM ships with a -15 dB Calibration Level."},
  {"title":"Meter ladder","page":"Gain Staging & Top-Down Calibration","file":"gain-staging.html","anchor":"meter-ladder","text":"Peak targets per stage. Read them on the REAPER MCP peak readout during the loudest chorus. Stage -24 -18 -12 -6 -3 0 Peak dBFS 01 Raw tracks & VSTi -12 to -10 02 Group buses -8 to -6 03 Master sub-groups -6 to -5 04 PRINT / MIX BUS in -4 to -3 05 MASTER ceiling -1.5 (TP -1.0)"},
  {"title":"Stage 01 procedure","page":"Gain Staging & Top-Down Calibration","file":"gain-staging.html","anchor":"stage-01","text":"Park faders Set all track and bus faders to 0.00 dB unity. Bypass track FX Hear the raw source only. Trim clip gain Drag the top edge of each audio item. Or press F2 and set Take volume. Turn down VSTi outputs Lower the master output on GGD, DjinnBass, ANA 2 and LABS. Read the meter Stop when the MCP"},
  {"title":"Four REAPER rules","page":"Gain Staging & Top-Down Calibration","file":"gain-staging.html","anchor":"rules","text":"Park all faders at 0.00 dB unity Never pull a fader down to fix a hot DI, vocal or synth preset. REAPER faders have maximum resolution near 0.00 dB . Trim pre-FX with clip gain or VSTi out Drag item top edges or use F2 Item Properties. Adjust VSTi output knobs or Slot 1 JS: Volume Adjustment . Target -12"},
  {"title":"Two-step mix bus calibration","page":"Gain Staging & Top-Down Calibration","file":"gain-staging.html","anchor":"calibration","text":"Calibrate the PRINT / MIX BUS top-down. Park the chain first. Then set compression on the first loud chorus."},
  {"title":"Step A: park the chain before sound plays","page":"Gain Staging & Top-Down Calibration","file":"gain-staging.html","anchor":"step-a","text":"1 Slate VCC / Coffee The Pun SAT VCC Brit 4k E or G, Drive 11:30 Coffee Preamp ON unverified , Bass / Treble 0 Use one or the other. Color only, no EQ moves at this stage. Options SLATE VCC Brit 4k E / Brit 4k G OTHER Coffee The Pun 2 VBC FG-Grey Slate COMP Ratio 2:1 unverified"},
  {"title":"Step B: first-chorus calibration","page":"Gain Staging & Top-Down Calibration","file":"gain-staging.html","anchor":"step-b","text":"Loop a loud chorus Keep Drums, Bass and Rhythm Guitars active. Pull down VBC FG-Grey Threshold Stop when the needle hits 2-3 dB GR on snare and kick hits. Gain-match Makeup Bypassed and active must sound equally loud. Check JST Maximizer #1 It must still read 0.0 dB GR . Step B targets FG-Grey GR 2-3 dB on snare/kick FG-Grey"},
  {"title":"Problem fix","page":"Gain Staging & Top-Down Calibration","file":"gain-staging.html","anchor":"problems","text":"Problem Fix Tool Hot DI, vocal or synth preset Trim pre-FX to -12 to -10 dBFS peak. Leave the fader at 0.00 dB . Clip gain (F2) / VSTi out / JS: Volume Adjustment Saturation overdrives early Raw tracks peak at -1 dBFS . Bring them down to -18 dBFS RMS . Clip gain / VSTi out Mix bus compressor"},
  {"title":"Guitars: Rhythm, Lead, Clean","page":"Guitars: Rhythm, Lead, Clean","file":"guitars.html","anchor":"","text":"A dry, locked 7-string rhythm wall built on JST Toneforge Disruptor . Leads sing in the center. Cleans sit wide in the hall."},
  {"title":"Three guitar buses","page":"Guitars: Rhythm, Lead, Clean","file":"guitars.html","anchor":"buses","text":"Bus Pan Sends & ducking soothe2 low cut RHYTHM GTR BUS 100% L/R Zero reverb or delay sends. Ducked by SC_SNARE (210 Hz & 2.8 kHz) and SC_SPOTLIGHT . 1.5-2.0 kHz LEAD GTR BUS Center 0% to 15% Pre-fader 0.0 dB SC_SPOTLIGHT . Post-fader VOX & LEAD PLATE and STEREO TEMPO DELAY . 600 Hz-1.0 kHz OCTAVE / MELODY GTR"},
  {"title":"Rhythm guitars","page":"Guitars: Rhythm, Lead, Clean","file":"guitars.html","anchor":"rhythm","text":"Hard-pan the 7-string rhythms 100% L/R . Keep them 100% dry . JW BG-Guitars with Chug Slayer tames Drop G-Bb palm-mute boom."},
  {"title":"Amp: JST Toneforge Disruptor","page":"Guitars: Rhythm, Lead, Clean","file":"guitars.html","anchor":"rhythm-amp","text":"A Toneforge Disruptor JST AMP Mode 7 STR, tuning to match the guitar Amplifier Protomech (high gain). Audition all five modes on a chug riff (starting point) Protomech Bass Below noon (starting point) Protomech Middle Above noon (starting point) Protomech Treble At or below noon (starting point) Battle For Utopia Reverb Bypassed High-Tech Delay Bypassed Fuel Injected Overdrive: Drive 0"},
  {"title":"Double or quad","page":"Guitars: Rhythm, Lead, Clean","file":"guitars.html","anchor":"rhythm-tracking","text":"Setup Pan & level Use when Double (2 takes) 100% L/R , equal level Default. Clearest pick attack. Best for fast djent rhythms. Quad (4 takes) Main pair 100% L/R . Second pair opposite-panned about 85% , lower in level Only when every take is tight. Loose quads sound washy and phasey. One player, both sides Adam Dutkiewicz has the"},
  {"title":"Post-amp chain on RHYTHM GTR BUS","page":"Guitars: Rhythm, Lead, Clean","file":"guitars.html","anchor":"rhythm-chain","text":"T Virtual Tape Machines (optional) Slate SAT Machine 2-Inch 16-Track Speed 30 ips Tape Type FG9 Bias Normal Noise Reduction Fully left (-80 dB, no hiss) Optional first slot. Tape softens fast pick transients and 4-8 kHz modeler fizz. 1 JST EQ JST EQ High Pass 85-100 Hz, 18-24 dB/oct unverified Low Pass 7.5-9 kHz Peak (narrow Q) -2 to"},
  {"title":"Vocal pocket: guitars keyed to the vocal","page":"Guitars: Rhythm, Lead, Clean","file":"guitars.html","anchor":"vocal-pocket","text":"The rhythm wall is the main thing that masks the lead vocal. Cut the guitars only while the singer sings. Key the cut from SC_SPOTLIGHT . Keep the cut in the mids. Chug lows and pick air stay untouched. Vocal pocket on RHYTHM GTR BUS (soothe2 sidechain) Sidechain ON, key = SC_SPOTLIGHT Stereo mode Mid/side, link 100% Stereo balance Toward"},
  {"title":"Lead guitars","page":"Guitars: Rhythm, Lead, Clean","file":"guitars.html","anchor":"leads","text":"Pan leads center, 0% to 15% . Keep pre-amp OD and modulation. Turn amp-sim reverb off. Turn delay off unless the riff needs it."},
  {"title":"LEAD GTR BUS chain","page":"Guitars: Rhythm, Lead, Clean","file":"guitars.html","anchor":"lead-chain","text":"A Archetype Petrucci Neural DSP AMP Amp reverb OFF Amp delay OFF unless riff-critical OD / modulation Keep Liquid sustain and focused 800 Hz mids. Notes sustain without turning to noise. Alternatives OTHER Archetype Nolly, Lead amp OTHER Archetype Gojira (pitch / whammy leads) JST Toneforge Disruptor 1 JST EQ JST EQ High Pass 100-120 Hz Low Pass 7-8 kHz"},
  {"title":"Lead sends","page":"Guitars: Rhythm, Lead, Clean","file":"guitars.html","anchor":"lead-sends","text":"LEAD GTR BUS sends Pre-fader 0.0 dB SC_SPOTLIGHT Post-fader VOX & LEAD PLATE Post-fader STEREO TEMPO DELAY Subtle VOX MICRO-PITCH & SLAP Delay option from the Slate guide: Repeater Space Delay, 1/4 left, dotted 1/8 right, HPF 300 Hz , LPF 4.5 kHz . Shared returns, not amp reverb A compressor on the shared return, keyed from the dry lead,"},
  {"title":"Cleans, octave & ambient","page":"Guitars: Rhythm, Lead, Clean","file":"guitars.html","anchor":"cleans","text":"Octave and melody parts pan 30-40% . They stay out of SC_SPOTLIGHT . The hall gives them space."},
  {"title":"OCTAVE / MELODY GTR chain","page":"Guitars: Rhythm, Lead, Clean","file":"guitars.html","anchor":"clean-chain","text":"A Archetype Nolly, Clean amp Neural DSP AMP Amp reverb OFF in the mix Keep reverb when The space is part of the part Transparent, punchy clean channel. The hall supplies the space. Alternatives OTHER Archetype Petrucci, Piezo OTHER Archetype Gojira, CLN JST Toneforge Disruptor 1 JST EQ JST EQ High Pass 130-150 Hz Low Pass 6.5-7.5 kHz Peak (scoop)"},
  {"title":"Ambient space","page":"Guitars: Rhythm, Lead, Clean","file":"guitars.html","anchor":"ambient-space","text":"Send 20-30% post-fader to WIDE MIX HALL . For swells and clean arpeggios, JST Sky Box Cloud or Shimmer fits. Keep amp-sim reverb only when that space is part of the composition."},
  {"title":"Problem fix","page":"Guitars: Rhythm, Lead, Clean","file":"guitars.html","anchor":"problems","text":"Problem Fix Tool Palm mutes boom and pump the mix bus Chug Slayer ON. Target 2-3 dB GR . Add the 130-140 Hz dynamic band. JW BG-Guitars / JST EQ Lead vocal disappears in the riffs Vocal pocket: mid-only cut keyed from SC_SPOTLIGHT . soothe2 / TDR Nova Quad rhythms sound washy Drop to a double. Or pull the second"},
  {"title":"WAP Mix Console","page":"WAP Mix Console","file":"index.html","anchor":"","text":""},
  {"title":"Target sound","page":"WAP Mix Console","file":"index.html","anchor":"target-sound","text":"Genre Modern metal. Big, full, melodic, polished. Tuning 7-string, Drop G to Bb. References Breaking Benjamin, Fire From The Gods, Red. Slightly djentier and lower. Vocals Howard Jones / AJ Channer style. Clarity first. Rhythm guitars Polished JST Toneforge Disruptor. Arrangement Orchestral strings in choruses. Aux synths and SFX throughout."},
  {"title":"Pages","page":"WAP Mix Console","file":"index.html","anchor":"pages","text":"Setup Gain Staging Per-stage dBFS targets and the top-down mix bus calibration. gain-staging.html Routing REAPER folder tree, bus layout and send structure. routing.html Mix Bus & Master Print bus chain and the MASTER limiter. mix-bus.html Tracks Drums Kick, snare, toms and cymbal chains. drums.html Bass Low end that locks to the kick. bass.html Guitars Rhythm and lead chains for low-tuned"},
  {"title":"Signal flow at a glance","page":"WAP Mix Console","file":"index.html","anchor":"signal-flow","text":"Two sub-groups sum into the print bus. The print bus feeds MASTER. Phase 3. Research applied to all setup and track pages. Values marked \"starting point\" or \"unverified\" need an ear check."},
  {"title":"Mix Bus & Master","page":"Mix Bus & Master","file":"mix-bus.html","anchor":"","text":"Glue at 2-3 dB GR . Tone with Max #1 at 0.0 dB GR . Print at -8.5 to -7.5 LUFS with true peak at or below -1.0 dBTP ."},
  {"title":"Two Maximizers, one polish stage","page":"Mix Bus & Master","file":"mix-bus.html","anchor":"concept","text":"Two JST Maximizer instances split the work. Max #1 on PRINT / MIX BUS is a dark tilt EQ only. Max #2 on MASTER sets loudness from a Rock genre preset. Soothe2 in Slot 4 is the only dynamic harshness stage. Do not stack TDR Nova on top of it. Park the chain and calibrate it first. Steps live on"},
  {"title":"PRINT / MIX BUS chain","page":"Mix Bus & Master","file":"mix-bus.html","anchor":"print-chain","text":"Input target: -4 to -3 dBFS peak. 1 VCC MixBuss / Coffee The Pun Slate / Acustica SAT VCC console Brit 4k E or G VCC drive 11:00-12:00 Coffee preamp ON (germanium) unverified Coffee Bass / Treble EQ 0 (flat) Console harmonics across the sum before compression. Use one or the other. Alternatives SLATE VCC Brit N SLATE VTM 1/2\""},
  {"title":"MASTER track","page":"Mix Bus & Master","file":"mix-bus.html","anchor":"master","text":"M JST Maximizer #2 JST LIMIT Preset Factory > Genre > Rock (or Heavy Rock) Maximize 2.5-4.5 dB GR on loud choruses Tone 0 (default) Threshold (output ceiling) -1.5 dB (starting point) OS 4x or 8x unverified Loudness -8.5 to -7.5 LUFS integrated True peak -1.0 dBTP max, read on the FG-X 2 meter Final metal loudness. The JST manual"},
  {"title":"Final print pass","page":"Mix Bus & Master","file":"mix-bus.html","anchor":"final-print","text":"Sweep Max #1 Tone Sweep Dark Light. GR must stay at 0.0 dB . Decide on Soothe2 Engage Slot 4 only if needed. Low cut band 250 Hz , Depth 0.8-1.2 . Set GClip Shave 1.0-2.0 dB of peaks. Raise Soothe2 quality Switch every Soothe2 instance to 4x Oversample unverified . Push Max #2 Drive to 2.5-4.5 dB GR ."},
  {"title":"Loudness targets and streaming","page":"Mix Bus & Master","file":"mix-bus.html","anchor":"loudness","text":"No public LUFS measurements exist for the reference records. The table uses the DR database logs (peak and RMS from the TT DR meter). The LUFS column is an estimate, not a measurement. Treat it as 1.5 dB. Release DR Peak RMS (loud tracks) LUFS estimate Breaking Benjamin, Dark Before Dawn (2015 CD) 6 0.00 dB -7.6 to -6.3 dB"},
  {"title":"Other full chains","page":"Mix Bus & Master","file":"mix-bus.html","anchor":"alt-chains","text":"Reference only. The page chain above wins. Each row is one complete PRINT / MIX BUS plus master from a guide. Flavor Chain Key numbers Slate hybrid VCC MixBuss > Coffee The Pun > FG-Grey > VTM > FG-X 2 FG-X 2 FG-Level 1.5-2.5 dB GR, Detail +0.6, LoPunch +0.8 unverified Stock + TDR SlickEQ > Kotelnikov > GClip >"},
  {"title":"Problem fix","page":"Mix Bus & Master","file":"mix-bus.html","anchor":"problems","text":"Problem Fix Tool Mix pumps on double kick Raise FG-Grey HPF into 90-100 Hz . VBC FG-Grey Max #1 shows gain reduction Back off Maximize to 0.0 dB GR . Recheck sub-group levels. JST Maximizer #1 Chorus turns harsh Engage Soothe2, Depth 0.8-1.2 . Never stack TDR Nova. Soothe2 Limiter distorts on snare hits Shave 1.0-2.0 dB with GClip first."},
  {"title":"Orchestral Strings","page":"Orchestral Strings","file":"orchestral.html","anchor":"","text":"Chorus strings hold sustained notes behind the guitars. Width carries them. WIDE MIX HALL supplies the tail."},
  {"title":"Lead or pad: pick one per section","page":"Orchestral Strings","file":"orchestral.html","anchor":"roles","text":"A string part does one job at a time. The job sets its register, level and reverb. Role When Writing Mix Pad Under a busy or syncopated vocal Slow whole and half notes. Divisi chords in violins 2 and violas. Back. More hall. Mid cut on. Counter-melody In gaps between vocal phrases Simple line. High violin register, often in octaves."},
  {"title":"Register vs Drop G-Bb guitars & vocal","page":"Orchestral Strings","file":"orchestral.html","anchor":"register","text":"Drop G sits at 49 Hz . Drop Bb sits at 58 Hz . The guitar body owns the low octaves. The vocal owns the octave it sings in. Section Where to write it Why Violins An octave or more above the vocal melody The high register cuts through a dense mix. It stays out of the vocal octave. Violas"},
  {"title":"String sources & built-in reverb","page":"Orchestral Strings","file":"orchestral.html","anchor":"sources-strings","text":"The manual builds chorus strings on Spitfire LABS . Violins sit wide. Cellos sit mid-wide. Spitfire LABS strings Reverb knob 0-15% unverified Violins Wide Cellos Mid-wide Output ORCH & SYNTH BUS A low reverb knob keeps the bow attack. The hall supplies the tail. Installed string sources with no settings in the sources: BBC Symphony Orchestra, Orchestral Strings, DSK Strings."},
  {"title":"Make LABS strings bigger","page":"Orchestral Strings","file":"orchestral.html","anchor":"bigger","text":"Double in octaves Put the line in violins and an octave down in cellos. Octave doubling is how arrangers lift a second chorus. Layer a second library on long notes only Add BBC Symphony Orchestra or DSK Strings under LABS longs. Doubling everything sounds massive but loses detail. This is a starting point. Taper the note ends Players fade a"},
  {"title":"String chain","page":"Orchestral Strings","file":"orchestral.html","anchor":"strings-chain","text":"1 VM Custom Opto Slate COMP Peak Reduction Set for 3-5 dB GR Speed Slow Sustained notes hold their level under chorus guitars. Sound On Sound used 3-4 dB of gentle compression on strings in a rock mix. It also made level rides easier. 2 JST EQ (Mid/Side) JST EQ High Pass 150-180 Hz (violins, violas, pads). Cellos with a"},
  {"title":"Routing & sends","page":"Orchestral Strings","file":"orchestral.html","anchor":"strings-sends","text":"String routing Output ORCH & SYNTH BUS Send 20-30% WIDE MIX HALL Shorts send Lower than longs. Starting point: half the longs send."},
  {"title":"Depth: front and back","page":"Orchestral Strings","file":"orchestral.html","anchor":"depth","text":"Pads sit back More hall send. Lower fader. Counter-melodies sit forward Less send. Ride the fader up in vocal gaps. Automate the send, not only the fader Raise the hall send at phrase ends and rests. Pull it down under held notes. Clear the center Sound On Sound cut the mid of the string reverb to free the vocal. See"},
  {"title":"Dynamics through the song","page":"Orchestral Strings","file":"orchestral.html","anchor":"automation","text":"Ride before you compress Automate the notes that jump out. A ride adds no tone of its own. Bring a section in on a rise Fade the strings in. Then lift them for the second half of the section. Narrow verses, wide choruses Keep verse strings narrower. Open them on the chorus downbeat. Cut a bright note, not the whole"},
  {"title":"EQ slotting vs guitars & vocals","page":"Orchestral Strings","file":"orchestral.html","anchor":"slotting","text":"Each part owns a band. Values come from each bus chain in the manual. Part HPF LPF Mid move Rhythm guitars 85-100 Hz 7.5-9 kHz +2 dB @ 1.8 kHz , notch 3.8-4.2 kHz Lead guitar 100-120 Hz 7-8 kHz +2 dB @ 1.2-2.5 kHz Octave / melody guitar 130-150 Hz 6.5-7.5 kHz -2 dB @ 1.5-2.5 kHz Lead vocal"},
  {"title":"Choir, brass & percussion","page":"Orchestral Strings","file":"orchestral.html","anchor":"brass-choir","text":"Installed: DSK ChoirZ, DSK Brass, DSK Overture, BBC Symphony Orchestra. No source gives settings for these instruments. Choir & brass starting points Choir HPF 150-200 Hz, steep. Higher if the arrangement is dense. Choir place Behind the BGVs. Same hall. Never in the lead vocal octave. Brass role Chorus hits and swells on chord roots. Starting point. Brass EQ Mid"},
  {"title":"ORCH & SYNTH BUS","page":"Orchestral Strings","file":"orchestral.html","anchor":"bus","text":"Strings and synths share one parent folder. It sums into INSTRUMENT BUS . SC_SPOTLIGHT ducks it through the parent folder. 1 JW BG-Keys JST COMP Push ON Crush 2-3 dB GR Loud Set to level-match Mix 80-100% Push adds parallel saturation and glue compression. It slightly widens the layers around the guitars. Crush is a limiter with auto-gain, so the"},
  {"title":"Problem fix","page":"Orchestral Strings","file":"orchestral.html","anchor":"problems","text":"Problem Fix Tool Strings mask the vocal Mid -2.5 dB @ 1.5-3 kHz . JST EQ (Mid/Side) Bow attack smears LABS reverb to 0-15% . Use the hall. Spitfire LABS Low-mid mud with guitars HPF 150-180 Hz . JST EQ Bow scrape is harsh Low cut band 350 Hz , Depth 2.0-3.0 , Soft. soothe2 Held notes drop under chorus"},
  {"title":"Plugins","page":"Plugins","file":"plugins.html","anchor":"","text":"Find any installed plugin, the guide pages that use it, and its official manual."},
  {"title":"Installed plugins","page":"Plugins","file":"plugins.html","anchor":"list","text":"From a scan of the mixing machine's VST2 and VST3 folders. \"Used on\" lists every guide page that names the plugin. Manual links open the vendor's site in a new tab. JavaScript is off The plugin list needs JavaScript. Each guide page names its plugins in the signal chain racks. Filter Vendor All vendors Category All categories Used in guide"},
  {"title":"Folder & Bus Routing","page":"Folder & Bus Routing","file":"routing.html","anchor":"","text":"Two-branch summing. INSTRUMENT BUS and VOCAL MASTER both feed PRINT / MIX BUS . Three silent hubs carry every sidechain."},
  {"title":"Signal flow schematic","page":"Folder & Bus Routing","file":"routing.html","anchor":"schematic","text":"All instrument buses live in one parent folder, INSTRUMENT BUS (\"ALL MUSIC\"). All vocal tracks and vocal FX returns live in VOCAL MASTER . Both folders route into PRINT / MIX BUS . Audio path Sidechain key (pre-fader, ch 3/4) Why two branches Vocals never hit the instrument glue. Screams cannot pump the guitar wall. Muting VOCAL MASTER prints a"},
  {"title":"Folder map","page":"Folder & Bus Routing","file":"routing.html","anchor":"folders","text":"Peak targets per folder. Full ladder on Gain Staging . Folder / bus Contains Peak target Chain INSTRUMENT BUS All non-vocal buses, WIDE MIX HALL -6 to -5 dBFS Receives SC_SPOTLIGHT duck DRUM BUS Kick, Snare, Toms, OH, Rooms, DRUM PARALLEL CRUSH , SNARE PLATE -8 to -6 dBFS FG-Grey 4:1 > GClip BASS BUS BASS LOW (<180 Hz), BASS"},
  {"title":"Silent sidechain hubs","page":"Folder & Bus Routing","file":"routing.html","anchor":"sidechain-hubs","text":"Each hub is a utility track with no output to the mix. It feeds plugin detectors only. Fader moves on the source never change the duck. Hub Fed by Keys Target GR SC_KICK Kick, pre-fader, 0.0 dB BASS LOW ducker (after ReaLimit) ANA 2 sub drops 2.5-3.5 dB 2-3 dB SC_SNARE Snare, pre-fader, 0.0 dB Optional dynamic dip on Rhythm"},
  {"title":"Hub setup in REAPER","page":"Folder & Bus Routing","file":"routing.html","anchor":"hub-setup","text":"Create the hub track Name it SC_KICK , SC_SNARE or SC_SPOTLIGHT . Uncheck Master/Parent Send Open the hub's Route window. The hub is now silent in the mix. Feed the hub Send the source track to the hub. Mode: Pre-Fader (Post-FX). Level: 0.0 dB . Set 4 channels on the target On the ducked track, set Track Channels from 2"},
  {"title":"SC_SPOTLIGHT duck on INSTRUMENT BUS","page":"Folder & Bus Routing","file":"routing.html","anchor":"spotlight","text":"1 TDR Nova Tokyo Dawn DYN EQ Key SC_SPOTLIGHT (Sidechain Input = EXT SC) Band 2.5-4 kHz, centered 3 kHz Target -1.5 to -2.5 dB Opens a pocket for the lead vocal and solos. The guitar wall keeps its body. Alternatives STOCK ReaComp, Auxiliary L+R detector"},
  {"title":"Where FX returns live","page":"Folder & Bus Routing","file":"routing.html","anchor":"fx-returns","text":"Return Sums into Why DRUM PARALLEL CRUSH DRUM BUS Drum bus glue hits dry and crush together. SNARE PLATE DRUM BUS Plate moves with the kit level. WIDE MIX HALL ORCH & SYNTH BUS (inside INSTRUMENT BUS ) Hall stays out of the vocal branch. VOX & LEAD PLATE VOCAL MASTER Mute VOCAL MASTER = no vocal tails in the"},
  {"title":"Do / do not","page":"Folder & Bus Routing","file":"routing.html","anchor":"guardrails","text":"Track / bus Do Do not Vocals (Lead Vox, BGVs) Route into VOCAL MASTER , then PRINT / MIX BUS . Never route into INSTRUMENT BUS . Never bypass PRINT / MIX BUS . Spatial FX returns Crush and Snare Plate to DRUM BUS . Hall to INSTRUMENT BUS . Vocal FX to VOCAL MASTER . Never put vocal plates"},
  {"title":"Problem fix","page":"Folder & Bus Routing","file":"routing.html","anchor":"problems","text":"Problem Fix Tool Vocals pump the guitar wall Move vocals out of INSTRUMENT BUS . Route them to VOCAL MASTER . REAPER folders Kick fader automation changes the bass duck Key the ducker from SC_KICK , fed pre-fader. Silent hub, ch 3/4 Kick suddenly louder after adding a hub Uncheck Master/Parent Send on the hub. Route window Lead vocal ducks"},
  {"title":"Synths & SFX","page":"Synths & SFX","file":"synths-sfx.html","anchor":"","text":"Atmosphere sits inside the shared returns. Built-in synth FX never clutter the low mids or collapse the width."},
  {"title":"Synth roles","page":"Synths & SFX","file":"synths-sfx.html","anchor":"roles","text":"Give each synth one role. The role sets its band, width and send. Role Band Width Send / route Pad HPF 250-400 Hz , scoop 1-3 kHz Wide WIDE MIX HALL Arp / pluck Delay High Pass 300 Hz Wide repeats, narrow dry STEREO TEMPO DELAY Riff reinforcement synth Low mids, shares the guitar band Narrow or mono when guitars"},
  {"title":"Built-in FX rules (ANA 2)","page":"Synths & SFX","file":"synths-sfx.html","anchor":"built-in-fx","text":"Strip or dial down preset reverbs and delays. Every layer then sits in WIDE MIX HALL or STEREO TEMPO DELAY . Part Internal reverb Internal delay / filter Compression Arps, plucks, synth bass 0% (OFF) Cut the delay slot Dry/Wet 50-70% , delay High Pass 300 Hz 0-2 dB GR max Pads, risers 15-25% max HPF 250-400 Hz , scoop"},
  {"title":"Pads & aux synths","page":"Synths & SFX","file":"synths-sfx.html","anchor":"pads","text":"1 JST EQ JST EQ High Pass 250-400 Hz Peak (scoop) -3 dB @ 1-3 kHz Clears low-mid room for guitars and bass. The scoop clears the vocal zone. Engineers report synth pads clash with heavy guitars near 1-2 kHz. A small high shelf lift can restore air after the scoop. Alternatives STOCK ReaEQ"},
  {"title":"Width","page":"Synths & SFX","file":"synths-sfx.html","anchor":"width","text":"Pads go wide Use ANA 2 Unison Width for spread at the source. Riff reinforcement synths stay narrow The guitars already fill the sides. A narrow synth keeps the stereo field clear. Lead synths sit center Widen them slightly so they leave the lead guitar room. Check mono Set kHs Stereo Width to 0%. The synth folds to mono for"},
  {"title":"Riff reinforcement & fuzz synths","page":"Synths & SFX","file":"synths-sfx.html","anchor":"fuzz-synth","text":"1 kHs Distortion Kilohearts SAT Type Overdrive or Saturate Drive Until the synth blends with the guitars. Starting point. Dynamics Raise to keep note dynamics Mix 100%, or lower for a cleaner verse A clean synth sounds like it is in a different room from distorted guitars. Grit puts it in the same space. Kane Churko and Will Carlson both"},
  {"title":"Arps & plucks","page":"Synths & SFX","file":"synths-sfx.html","anchor":"arps","text":"Internal reverb off. Delay Dry/Wet cut and delay High Pass at 300 Hz . Send arps and plucks to STEREO TEMPO DELAY ."},
  {"title":"Synth sends","page":"Synths & SFX","file":"synths-sfx.html","anchor":"synth-sends","text":"Synth sends Pads WIDE MIX HALL Arps / plucks STEREO TEMPO DELAY Reinforcement synths Dry Output ORCH & SYNTH BUS"},
  {"title":"Risers, impacts & sub drops","page":"Synths & SFX","file":"synths-sfx.html","anchor":"risers-impacts","text":""},
  {"title":"Placement rules","page":"Synths & SFX","file":"synths-sfx.html","anchor":"sfx-rules","text":"Effect Where it lands Level Reverse cymbal Peak just before the downbeat it leads into Audible in the build. Gone at the hit. Riser Ends on or just before the new section Ramp from silence. Cut off as the section starts. Impact / explosion On the \"one\" where the full band re-enters Quiet, under kick and snare Sub drop Front"},
  {"title":"Risers","page":"Synths & SFX","file":"synths-sfx.html","anchor":"risers","text":"Treat risers like pads. Internal reverb 15-25% max. HPF 250-400 Hz . Compression 0-2 dB GR max. Static HPF plus moving LPF The HPF keeps the build off the kick and bass. An LPF that opens across the build adds the lift. Shave the top of the tail Noise risers get harsh at the peak. Reverse cymbals Use a one-shot"},
  {"title":"Sub drops","page":"Synths & SFX","file":"synths-sfx.html","anchor":"sub-drops","text":"Sub drops Width Mono below 150 Hz Compression 3-5 dB GR Duck 2-3 dB by SC_KICK Pitch Tune to the riff root. Fast pitch envelope down. The kick ducks the drop so both keep their punch. 1 ReaEQ (HPF sweep) REAPER EQ Type High pass Frequency Start near 150 Hz. Lower it until the weight returns without masking kick or"},
  {"title":"Impacts & transitions","page":"Synths & SFX","file":"synths-sfx.html","anchor":"impacts","text":"1 ReaEQ (HPF sweep) REAPER EQ Type High pass Frequency Start near 150 Hz, then lower it Explosion samples carry heavy sub. Same sweep method as the sub drop. 2 kHs Transient Shaper Kilohearts TRANS Attack Slight boost Sustain Cut until the tail clears the next snare. Starting point. Clip On A short impact hits the downbeat and gets out"},
  {"title":"Glitches & stutters","page":"Synths & SFX","file":"synths-sfx.html","anchor":"glitch","text":"Plugin Controls Use kHs Tape Stop Play, Stop Time, Start Time, Curve Slows a part to a stop before a hard break. Automate Play. kHs Trance Gate Pattern Editor, Resolution, Length, Mix Rhythmic chops on a pad or vocal tail in a build. kHs Reverser Delay time, Sync, Crossfade, Mix Plays reversed slices of the input. Raise Crossfade to stop"},
  {"title":"Transition automation","page":"Synths & SFX","file":"synths-sfx.html","anchor":"transitions","text":"Dip, then hit Pull the mix down about 1.5 dB over the last 1-2 bars. Return to 0 dB on the downbeat. Close a low-pass, then open it Close a kHs Filter (Type low pass) on the instrument bus across the build. Bypass it on the hit. Thin, then fill Raise the guitar and bass HPF during the build. Drop"},
  {"title":"ORCH & SYNTH BUS","page":"Synths & SFX","file":"synths-sfx.html","anchor":"bus","text":"Synths share the strings bus: JW BG-Keys 2-3 dB GR , optional soothe2 with its low cut band at 300-500 Hz . The BG-Keys Crush limiter caps synth filter-sweep peaks. Full chain: Orchestral, ORCH & SYNTH BUS . SFX can run on their own folder into INSTRUMENT BUS . That keeps impacts out of the BG-Keys limiter. Starting point."},
  {"title":"Problem fix","page":"Synths & SFX","file":"synths-sfx.html","anchor":"problems","text":"Problem Fix Tool Low-mid delay clutter Cut the delay Dry/Wet 50-70% . Set the delay High Pass to 300 Hz . ANA 2 Stereo width collapses Internal reverb off or 15-25% max. Use the shared hall. ANA 2 / WIDE MIX HALL Sub drop fights the kick Mono below 150 Hz . Duck 2-3 dB from the kick. SC_KICK Saw"},
  {"title":"Vocals: Lead, Screams, Stacks","page":"Vocals: Lead, Screams, Stacks","file":"vocals.html","anchor":"","text":"Clarity first. A dead-mono lead core sits in front of Drop G-Bb guitars. BGVs wrap wide around it inside VOCAL MASTER ."},
  {"title":"Vocal buses","page":"Vocals: Lead, Screams, Stacks","file":"vocals.html","anchor":"buses","text":"Bus Pan Sends Parent LEAD VOX BUS Mono center Pre-fader SC_SPOTLIGHT . Post-fader plate, slap, tempo delay. VOCAL MASTER BGV / HARMONY 85-100% L/R Post-fader VOX & LEAD PLATE , WIDE MIX HALL . VOCAL MASTER HPF before Coffee The Pun Always put an 85-100 Hz HPF before Coffee The Pun. Sub rumble and plosives otherwise drive the preamp model"},
  {"title":"Clean lead","page":"Vocals: Lead, Screams, Stacks","file":"vocals.html","anchor":"clean-lead","text":"One chain serves the whole LEAD VOX BUS . Slots 1-6 are the manual chain. Slots P1, P2 and F come from the guides and are optional."},
  {"title":"LEAD VOX BUS chain","page":"Vocals: Lead, Screams, Stacks","file":"vocals.html","anchor":"lead-chain","text":"P1 MetaTune (optional) Slate PITCH Speed 15 ms Amount 85% Note Stabilizer Mid (ignores notes under 80 ms) Centers pitch drift before any dynamics. Note Stabilizer ignores short passing notes. It adds 280 ms latency. Alternatives OTHER Graillon 2 P2 E2Deesser Eiosis DE-ESS Position First, or right after tuning Mode Solo Vocal Targets s, z, t, sh Splits the signal"},
  {"title":"Screams","page":"Vocals: Lead, Screams, Stacks","file":"vocals.html","anchor":"screams","text":"Screams run the same LEAD VOX BUS chain. Change only these settings. Setting Clean lead Screams soothe2 Mode Soft Hard (bright screams only) MetaTune Speed 15 ms 15-25 ms , Note Stabilizer Mid De-esser E2Deesser SSL DeEss: fast VCA bite on high-frequency spit JW BG-Vocals mode No mode switch No mode switch. Use Crush and Aggro. JW BG-Vocals GR 3-5"},
  {"title":"Doubles & harmonies","page":"Vocals: Lead, Screams, Stacks","file":"vocals.html","anchor":"doubles","text":""},
  {"title":"Lead doubles","page":"Vocals: Lead, Screams, Stacks","file":"vocals.html","anchor":"lead-doubles","text":"Do not double every line. Erik Ron skips doubles on many parts. A single lead reads closer and clearer in verses. Save doubles for choruses and big screams. Lead doubles Verses Single lead, dead center Tucked double Pan about 1 o'clock (about 30% R), under the lead Chorus stereo pair Two doubles, 85-100% L/R, for size without a second singer"},
  {"title":"BGV / HARMONY chain","page":"Vocals: Lead, Screams, Stacks","file":"vocals.html","anchor":"bgv-chain","text":"1 JST EQ JST EQ High Pass 120-140 Hz Low Pass 8-9 kHz Thins the stack so it never crowds the lead core. Alternatives STOCK ReaEQ 2 JW BG-Vocals JST COMP Crush 4-6 dB GR Aggro 15-25% Glues the stack into one wide layer. The Crush limiter catches plosive and sibilance peaks. Alternatives JST JST BG-Vocals, Blend mode 3 MetaTune"},
  {"title":"Placement & sends","page":"Vocals: Lead, Screams, Stacks","file":"vocals.html","anchor":"bgv-placement","text":"BGV / HARMONY placement Pan 85-100% L/R Width Wide pan or micro-pitch Post-fader VOX & LEAD PLATE Post-fader WIDE MIX HALL Parent VOCAL MASTER"},
  {"title":"Throws & sends","page":"Vocals: Lead, Screams, Stacks","file":"vocals.html","anchor":"throws","text":""},
  {"title":"Lead vox sends","page":"Vocals: Lead, Screams, Stacks","file":"vocals.html","anchor":"lead-sends","text":"LEAD VOX BUS sends Pre-fader SC_SPOTLIGHT Post-fader VOX & LEAD PLATE Post-fader VOX MICRO-PITCH & SLAP (Bendeth slap: 80 ms) Post-fader STEREO TEMPO DELAY (automated)"},
  {"title":"Delay throws","page":"Vocals: Lead, Screams, Stacks","file":"vocals.html","anchor":"delay-throws","text":"Automate the send Draw send-level automation on LEAD VOX BUS STEREO TEMPO DELAY . Raise it on throw words Lift the send only on the phrases you want repeated. Let the duck work The return ducks 3-4 dB from SC_SPOTLIGHT . Repeats bloom in the gaps. STEREO TEMPO DELAY (shared return) Time 1/4 or 1/8 dotted Ping-Pong ON Feedback L"},
  {"title":"Problem fix","page":"Vocals: Lead, Screams, Stacks","file":"vocals.html","anchor":"problems","text":"Problem Fix Tool Plosives pump the compressors HPF 85-100 Hz before Coffee The Pun. JST EQ / ReaEQ Raw mic sounds boxy Peak -2 dB @ 240-300 Hz . JST EQ Chest mud near 300 Hz Boost and Atten together at 100 Hz . Coffee The Pun Throat bite at 2.5-4.5 kHz Low cut band 350-400 Hz , Depth from"}
